[
  {
    "id": "furthest-quintiles-moved",
    "source": "As the chart above shows, middle income families are actually quite mobile. Nearly all families move at least one quintile during their lifetimes, with nearly half moving two quintiles. Interestingly, more middle income families moved four quintiles - meaning they experienced both the top and bottom quintiles at least once - while fewer families shifted up or down only one quintile from the middle.",
    "claim": "The furthest number of quintiles moved is two for 41.8% of families, three for 34.4%, four for 18.3%, one for 5.4% and none for 0.1%.",
    "tables": {
      "d": "data/line_chart_data.csv"
    },
    "code": "d.assign(fam=d.id.astype(str)+'_'+(d.groupby('id',sort=False).year.diff().le(0)).groupby(d.id).cumsum().astype(str)).groupby('fam').quintile.agg(np.ptp).value_counts(normalize=True).mul(100).round(1).sort_index()"
  },
  {
    "id": "cumulative-years-in-each-quintile",
    "source": "Just watch as we draw one line per family and count up how many years the 7,857 families that we have at least ten years of data on have cumulatively spent at each income level.",
    "claim": "Cumulative years are 23,163 in quintile 1, 35,140 in quintile 2, 42,720 in quintile 3, 40,583 in quintile 4 and 29,464 in quintile 5.",
    "tables": {
      "d": "data/line_chart_data.csv"
    },
    "code": "d.quintile.value_counts().sort_index()"
  }
]

[
  {
    "source": "Generally speaking, white families experience more cumulative years above the middle quintile in either the upper-middle or upper income levels (50% of cumulative years) than below it. In contrast, Black and Latino families spend more time below the middle quintile in either lower-middle or lower income levels (50%, and 40% of cumulative years, respectively).",
    "why": "The file has no race or ethnicity column, so cumulative years cannot be split by race."
  },
  {
    "source": "White families 9% 17% 25% 27% 22% (across 4,784 families); Black families 23% 27% 25% 17% 7% (across 2,716 families); Latino families 14% 26% 28% 20% 12% (across 467 families)",
    "why": "The file has no race or ethnicity column; the race charts are also computed over the larger 11,172-family sample, not the 7,857 families in this file."
  },
  {
    "source": "A study released in 2018, focused specifically on mobility of children compared to their parents, found that even when Black and white boys both started life at the highest levels of wealth, white boys were more likely to remain at that income level. In contrast, Black boys were much more likely to end up in the lower income levels as adults.",
    "why": "This is a finding reported from an external study, not computed from line_chart_data.csv."
  },
  {
    "source": "Another study found that income gaps between Black and white Americans have only become slightly narrower overall since 1970, whereas Hispanic families are now further from earning incomes equivalent to white families than they were in 1970.",
    "why": "This is a finding reported from an external study, not computed from line_chart_data.csv."
  },
  {
    "source": "the 2018 State of Working America Wages reports that wages for white workers continued to grow at a faster rate than wages for both Black or Hispanic workers since 2000.",
    "why": "This is a finding reported from an external report, not computed from line_chart_data.csv."
  },
  {
    "source": "white family wealth was seven times greater than [B]lack family wealth and five times greater than Hispanic family wealth in 2016.",
    "why": "This is a finding reported from The Urban Institute, not computed from line_chart_data.csv."
  }
]

